hi my name is Rob reggae Schofield I am  a user interface architect for Wolfram  research my talk is titled Wolfram  player for iOS and I will be introducing  the iOS version of the Wolfram player  app we recently introduced that is  available now for iPads and iPhones  all right I'd like to thank all of you  for coming my name is Robert guy  Scofield I work at Wolfram research in  the user-interface Department and I am  one of the members of a team who has  been working for a bit of time on this  product that we have just released  called Wolfram Wolfram player for iOS  and that is the app that you're looking  at on the screen right now one thing  that's going to you know be a fairly  significant difference between this talk  and a talk you may have seen me give at  a previous conference is that I'm not  using an app that I built myself on my  computer and transferred onto my iPad  I'm using the app that is available in  the App Store right now and anyone can  go here and download this it's a free  download so I highly recommend everyone  do that and I was just noticing that I  haven't given it a review so I'm just  gonna go ahead and give it a review real  quick so right  this is Wolfram Player and as I said  this is now a released app we've been  working on it for a number of years  several months ago we made it available  to many people who maybe in this room as  a as a beta and we thank many of you for  participating in that program and  submitting to us some valuable feedback  we've been working our way through lots  and lots of issues over the past several  months and as of two weeks ago the app  was finally released and it will run on  both an iPad or iPhone as long as it's  running iOS 9.3 or higher and it is as I  said a free download so you the free app  that you get will display the contents  of arbitrary CDF documents which is  possibly a little bit different from  what I had described in the past  so even dynamic content will now display  for you in the free version whereas  previously dynamic content was just kind  of blocked  now the dynamic content is displayed for  free  you can't immediately interact with it  but I'll get to that in just a second  but you can view and even rotate 3d  graphics in the three vert and the free  version also so if you want to enable  full dynamic interactivity this is  something that's optional and you can do  it in one of three ways that I have  listed here so you can work with  Enterprise CDF documents which is a way  to author the documents so if you have  an Enterprise version of Mathematica  which I think costs a little bit more  money you can sign these CDF documents  and then anyone can look at them and  interact with them for free the second  option is to subscribe to Wolfram cloud  and that will once you log into your  Wolfram cloud account in the app that  will enable all dynamic interactivity  and the third option is just a one-time  in-app purchase about $10 u.s. and that  will enable all dynamic interactivity  for any document for your account so  this app has a number of interesting  features so you can open documents from  anywhere we allow you to log into  Wolfram cloud accounts directly in the  app and I can just give you an example  of that if I go back here this document  that I'm viewing is actually Ana Wolfram  a cloud account so there's a few  different sections I have some locally  stored documents I have a couple of  Wolfram cloud accounts that I'm signed  into you and I can just view all my  files navigate through the folder  hierarchy and go to the file I want and  Here I am you can download files from  the web and if anyone was in the room  before the talk started you may have  seen me do that a couple times I went to  the demonstrations dot Wolfram comm and  was able to go find a demonstration that  I want to look at I'm going to tap the  download link and it's going to ask me  if I want to open that Wolfram player  and I say  yes of course and then I can just view  the file immediately and this is now  stored locally so I can come back to it  if I want without having to go back to  the web the same works for web downloads  also works for email attachments also  works for other cloud services iCloud  Dropbox Google Drive any of those if you  have a CDF or notebook file stored on  any of those services you can get to  them in the app and open the document  directly and then you can also sync  documents with iTunes which is a little  bit more complicated and cumbersome  process but it's the one way to do it  without using some sort of network cloud  service so what what is this app do it  does local computations so the wolfram  engine is embedded inside the app it  doesn't need a network connection to do  any of the computations everything  everything is done locally so the  benefit of that is improve performance  and you can do it when you're offline so  we have user interface and experience  that are appropriate for the platform so  when we have tap targets things like  buttons for instance they're all going  to be finger sized so you can get your  finger on them to tap them you don't  have to necessarily have a very precise  location for a very imprecise pointing  device and the manipulate controls by  default will be positioned at the bottom  of the screen so that you're not having  to cover the content with your hand and  kind of have to look around your hand to  to see what you're actually manipulating  but there's a way to get around that if  that doesn't work for your particular  application so it supports all of the  features of computable documents  typesetting  and I have this the font size there we  go  [Music]  graphics and there these aren't static  bitmaps that are rendered at one size we  can zoom this and it continues to look  nice  as we zoom in as far as we can get here  graphics 3d same sort of thing and this  has the also the feature where we can  rotate the 3d graphics we can toss them  so you can get it going and that will  keep going for a little bit we can use  well so just a single single finger will  rotate it and then you can toss it  yeah no controls and then you can do two  finger gestures to pan the graphic  around and you can pinch to zoom in to  the graphic and as I mentioned earlier  3d graphics viewing and interacting with  them both available in the free version  of Wolfram player so dynamic content  this will you can view and interact with  dynamic content same as you can on a  desktop system if you want to interact  with the dynamic content and what I mean  is to tap on this checkbox and have it  respond to the tab that is what requires  the enhanced functionality which I  mentioned earlier you can get through an  Enterprise CDF or a cloud subscription  or an in-app purchase so I can tap on  this and the document will respond to it  and of course that includes everyone's  favorite manipulate so we tap on the  manipulate and we get controls at the  bottom here and we can interact with the  control without covering it  and there are some features that are  specific to touch that aren't  necessarily meaningful and a desktop  system so on desktop system we have a  function called Mouse position which  always returns a list of XY coordinates  on a touchscreen that's not super useful  because there may not be a current touch  happening or there could be more than  one so we have a new function called  touch position which is an analog to  Mouse position but it could return zero  coordinates or it could return I  actually don't know what the maximum is  but I have tested it and I can  definitely get all 10 fingers on  and then there is an option to  manipulate a which is similar to the  existing control placement option which  we kind of ignore on a touchscreen  because many manipulates authored with  this option we're kind of created  without thinking about a touchscreen so  by default we put the controls at the  bottom if you have a different control  placement that would be better in in  your specific case like here I have an  example that just uses a vertical slider  and I've decided I didn't want to put a  vertical slider at the bottom of the  screen and have it take up you know a  bar all the way across when it's a very  narrow tall thing so I'm going to put it  at the side of the screen instead and I  can do that with this touchscreen  control placement arrow right and I can  [Music]  have my same manipulate with the  controls in a different place there so  we certainly aim for this to be as  compatible as possible with desktop  versions of Mathematica and one thing  that all just note real quick if you  look at the you know the version  information this is version 11.10 clear  for iOS but this is really a version one  product this is kind of a brand new  thing and so there are some ways which  it is not 100% compatible with the  desktop versions of wolfram player so  some of them some of the limitations  that we know about since this is a  player app does not allow content  creation it only allows you to view and  interact with the existing content so  you still need to use either your  desktop version or Wolfram cloud to  create new content and we do have a  Wolfram cloud app for iOS which if you  need to create something on your iOS  device you can use the Wolfram cloud app  and then if you want to interact with  that content in a way that's offline  and/or potentially with  better performance due to the local  computations then you can download it  into the player app and interact with it  there yes yeah  yes you can so we do have support for  input field and as I mentioned in just a  moment it is somewhat more limited than  what it is in the desktop version and  I'll get to that real quick yeah yes if  you look in the title bar the document  that I am using right now is an NB file  so the the one way it makes a  distinction is if you have an enterprise  signed CDF file that will allow you to  do dynamic interactivity for free and  any other file won't ok so we had we do  have some incomplete features that we  know about one is slide shows which I'm  sure many of you have noticed I'm not  using a slide show to display this which  is a little bit unfortunate but we know  about it and it's something that's high  on our priority list to get fixed as  soon as possible  things like event handler we don't have  good support for certain examples of  locator if it's outside of a manipulate  we don't support that at the moment  input field we allow entering of plain  text of numbers but what we don't have  support for is typesetting inside an  input field and things like J link and  database link these are built on top of  Java and these are things that are never  going to work on the iOS version wolfram  player and then one other thing to  consider is performance I have given a  talk somewhat similar to this for a few  years at the conference now and over  that time  I used to say you know be prepared the  performance is going to be nowhere near  what it is on a desktop system and  within the last two years that's kind of  changed dramatically the speed of new  iOS devices in the last two years is  pretty remarkable especially compared to  what it was in the past I have a an  iPhone 7 which has a faster CPU than my  wife's MacBook the new iPhones that were  just released have a faster CPU than a  lot of the MacBook Pros that Apple is  currently selling brand-new so this used  to be a big issue it's less of an issue  anymore especially if you have a device  that's you know less than 2 years old if  you have an older device you could still  run into some performance problems and a  lot of these devices you know keep  running year after year after year and  so people don't necessarily upgrade them  so that's something to think about  you may not get super smooth animation  or you know interactivity if you have an  iPad 3 for example and the older write  devices also had a pretty small amount  of RAM so if you're doing computations  in the wolfram engine that use a lot of  RAM sometimes it runs out and there are  cases where we handle that more  gracefully than others sometimes the  Apple will be just killed by the  operating system and there's not really  anything we can do about it  so if that happens for inexplicable  reasons but you have reason to believe  you know maybe this computation was  using a lot of memory that would be one  way to look at going about trying to to  fix that did you have a question ok  okay I will just say in this particular  app on iOS that isn't an issue because  there's only one in Turkey and we don't  support shift in ur evaluations at all  so the enter is the one and only thing  that we can do sure and one of the  limitation as I'm sure several people  noticed from Stevens talk last night he  killed the kernel a few times when he  was having problems that's not something  that we can really do in the app right  now we can't restart the Wolfram engine  from within the same process at the  moment so if your Wolfram engine runs  away out of control then you kill the  app and you'll have to bring the UI  backup also so we think you know we  spend a fair bit of time on it we think  what we have now available to everyone  is a pretty solid product we're quite  happy with it but as I said this is a  1.0 version despite the fact that it's  version 11 point 2 point 1 but we  received a lot of valuable feedback  during the beta process but since we  released it to the general public that  has increased significantly and we've  just been receiving tons of really  useful feedback lots of things where the  app didn't work is expected or something  didn't you know up here on the screen  exactly the same way as it does on  desktop and people have been reporting  those issues to us and we've been fixing  them so we are going to have more  releases fixing lots of these types of  bugs we've already fixed dozens and  dozens of user reported bugs since the  release two weeks ago we have a version  coming out that's going to use the  Wolfram engine version 11 point 2 right  now it's based on eleven point one so  all the new features in eleven point two  will be available and as I said  slideshow support is something that's  high on our list and  these are things that we all hope to do  by the end of the year and that is a  pretty realistic expectation I think so  what you all can what you all can do is  get the app if you haven't done it  already install the app on your iPhone  or iPad from the App Store you just have  to go to the app store and search for  wolfram player it will show up you can  get it for free you're going to most  likely want to enable full interactivity  as I mentioned we have three ways of  doing that you can work with Enterprise  CDF this is CDF documents created by an  enterprise version of Mathematica those  are interactivity is available to  everyone with those documents regardless  of whether they have a cloud  subscription or an in-app purchase you  can get a cloud subscription that will  enable interactivity in your app once  you sign in with your cloud account that  has this subscription or you can do the  one-time in app purchase in these other  ways you can enable dynamic  interactivity once you do that you can  take your content with you  through any number of ways I mentioned  earlier you can download them through a  web browser through a cloud file system  like iCloud Dropbox Google Drive you can  get them through email you can sync them  with iTunes anything like that take your  documents with you and then please if  you see problems you see things that  don't look right report those issues to  us and we will do our best to improve  the compatibility

